1
00:00:00,067 --> 00:00:00,890
Olive drab.

2
00:00:00,930 --> 00:00:05,994
It's the color on almost every American bomb and artillery shell.

3
00:00:06,034 --> 00:00:09,135
And on its own, it means almost nothing.

4
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:11,095
It's only the base coat.

5
00:00:11,135 --> 00:00:17,108
Under the military's ammunition color code, the real message is painted on top, as a stripe.

6
00:00:17,533 --> 00:00:21,887
That code covers ammunition twenty millimeters and larger.

7
00:00:21,927 --> 00:00:25,978
Smaller bullets use a different system: a painted tip.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:33,834
So once you can read the stripes, a pile of identical green bombs suddenly starts telling you exactly what's inside.

9
00:00:34,567 --> 00:00:35,083
Yellow.

10
00:00:35,123 --> 00:00:37,862
A yellow band means high explosive.

11
00:00:38,267 --> 00:00:42,766
Take the Mark eighty-two, the five-hundred-pound general purpose bomb.

12
00:00:42,806 --> 00:00:46,139
Almost two hundred pounds of it is explosive filler.

13
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:52,334
A live one is olive drab, with a yellow band about three inches wide around the nose.

14
00:00:52,767 --> 00:00:55,303
And some bombs get two yellow bands.

15
00:00:55,343 --> 00:00:57,521
That second stripe has a story.

16
00:00:57,933 --> 00:01:04,665
On July twenty-ninth, nineteen sixty-seven, a fire broke out on the deck of the carrier U S S Forrestal.

17
00:01:04,705 --> 00:01:09,223
A bomb sitting in the flames exploded about a minute and a half later.

18
00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:13,498
After that, the Navy pushed for bombs that could survive a fire.

19
00:01:13,538 --> 00:01:20,392
With a heat-resistant coating, a Mark eighty-two can sit in burning fuel for several minutes before it cooks off.

20
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:25,562
Those thermally protected bombs are the ones with two yellow bands.

21
00:01:25,602 --> 00:01:27,496
One stripe means it's live.

22
00:01:27,536 --> 00:01:31,868
Two stripes mean it's live, and it was built to buy you time.

23
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:33,194
Brown.

24
00:01:33,234 --> 00:01:35,943
Brown means low explosive.

25
00:01:36,367 --> 00:01:37,504
Not the blast.

26
00:01:37,544 --> 00:01:38,261
The push.

27
00:01:38,301 --> 00:01:41,457
Propellant charges, and rocket motors.

28
00:01:41,867 --> 00:01:46,613
A low explosive burns from layer to layer, slower than the speed of sound.

29
00:01:46,653 --> 00:01:53,340
A high explosive detonates, with a shock wave that can race through it at several kilometers per second.

30
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:55,598
One is controlled pressure.

31
00:01:55,638 --> 00:01:57,678
The other is shattering force.

32
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:05,117
So a brown band on a missile tells the crew loading it that the motor is live, even if the warhead has a code of its own.

33
00:02:05,833 --> 00:02:06,672
Light blue.

34
00:02:06,712 --> 00:02:08,748
Light blue means practice.

35
00:02:09,167 --> 00:02:15,557
The B D U thirty-three is a blue, twenty-five-pound practice bomb, shaped to fly like a Mark eighty-two.

36
00:02:15,967 --> 00:02:17,983
But blue does not mean harmless.

37
00:02:18,023 --> 00:02:24,023
Inside is a spotting charge that throws up a puff of smoke, so crews can score where they hit.

38
00:02:24,433 --> 00:02:30,547
In July twenty nineteen, an A ten hit a bird over Georgia and accidentally released three of them.

39
00:02:30,587 --> 00:02:33,373
They came down somewhere in northern Florida.

40
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:37,753
The Air Force's advice to anyone who found one was simple.

41
00:02:37,793 --> 00:02:38,993
Don't approach it.

42
00:02:39,033 --> 00:02:41,058
Mark the spot, and call it in.

43
00:02:41,467 --> 00:02:48,418
And there are hundreds of thousands of these practice bombs sitting on American bombing ranges right now.

44
00:02:49,133 --> 00:02:50,216
Light green.

45
00:02:50,256 --> 00:02:52,462
Light green means smoke.

46
00:02:52,867 --> 00:02:55,737
Screening smoke, to hide troops and vehicles.

47
00:02:55,777 --> 00:02:59,488
Or marking smoke, to point out a target or a landing zone.

48
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:06,662
The classic M eighteen smoke grenade comes in four colors: red, yellow, green, and violet.

49
00:03:06,702 --> 00:03:10,138
It pours out colored smoke for about a minute.

50
00:03:10,567 --> 00:03:14,577
Violet was once pulled, because the original formula was toxic.

51
00:03:14,617 --> 00:03:17,791
A safer version only arrived in the two thousands.

52
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:21,938
This kind of round is built to hide things, not to destroy them.

53
00:03:22,667 --> 00:03:23,156
White.

54
00:03:23,196 --> 00:03:25,360
White means illumination.

55
00:03:25,767 --> 00:03:28,261
These rounds aren't meant to hit anything.

56
00:03:28,301 --> 00:03:33,813
High above the battlefield, the shell pushes out a flare that drifts down under a parachute.

57
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:41,623
A modern one hundred fifty-five millimeter illumination round burns at about one million candlepower, for around two minutes.

58
00:03:42,033 --> 00:03:46,764
For two minutes, a few square kilometers of night turn into something close to day.

59
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:48,039
Black.

60
00:03:48,079 --> 00:03:50,743
Black means armor defeating.

61
00:03:51,167 --> 00:03:53,856
These are the rounds built to go through tanks.

62
00:03:54,267 --> 00:04:04,120
A modern American tank sabot round leaves the barrel at over fifteen hundred meters per second, a dense metal dart that relies on speed, not explosives.

63
00:04:04,533 --> 00:04:10,067
It's the same idea as the black tip on an armor piercing bullet, just scaled up to a tank gun.

64
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:11,521
And gray.

65
00:04:11,561 --> 00:04:14,566
Gray is the one nobody wants to see.

66
00:04:14,606 --> 00:04:16,849
A gray body means chemical.

67
00:04:17,267 --> 00:04:19,701
The band tells you which kind.

68
00:04:19,741 --> 00:04:23,659
Dark red is a riot control agent, like tear gas.

69
00:04:23,699 --> 00:04:26,462
Violet is an incapacitating agent.

70
00:04:26,502 --> 00:04:29,019
Dark green is a toxic chemical.

71
00:04:29,433 --> 00:04:39,912
And if that dark green band is broken into half-inch dashes, it marks a binary nerve agent: two chemicals stored apart, that only combine after the round is fired.

72
00:04:40,333 --> 00:04:47,143
America's binary sarin shell, the M six eighty-seven, went into production in nineteen eighty-seven.

73
00:04:47,183 --> 00:04:49,061
It was never used in combat.

74
00:04:49,467 --> 00:04:59,126
And on July seventh, twenty twenty-three, at the Blue Grass Army Depot in Kentucky, the United States destroyed the last weapon in its declared chemical stockpile.

75
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:04,191
So the gray shell with the green band is now a color you mostly find in a manual.

76
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:05,751
One warning.

77
00:05:05,791 --> 00:05:09,688
Colors fade, and other countries use their own codes.

78
00:05:09,728 --> 00:05:13,104
A color is never proof that something is safe.

79
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:18,105
But on an American flight line, yellow is the blast.

80
00:05:18,145 --> 00:05:19,702
Brown is the push.

81
00:05:19,742 --> 00:05:21,210
Blue is practice.

82
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:22,540
Green is smoke.

83
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:23,870
White is light.

84
00:05:23,910 --> 00:05:25,201
Black is armor.

85
00:05:25,241 --> 00:05:29,901
And gray is the one you hope never leaves the bunker.

86
00:05:30,333 --> 00:05:32,441
Which stripe surprised you the most?

87
00:05:32,481 --> 00:05:33,872
Tell me in the comments.
